1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,480
How long can the police keep

2
00:00:01,480 --> 00:00:02,954
you at the side of the road?

3
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:06,705
Not why they can stop you —

4
00:00:06,706 --> 00:00:10,081
how long they can hold you once they have.
Just after midnight on a dark Nebraska

5
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:12,400
highway, a driver drifts onto the shoulder

6
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:14,460
for a second, and a patrol car

7
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:18,201
lights up behind him.

8
00:00:18,202 --> 00:00:21,911
The officer checks his papers, writes a warning,
hands everything back. The stop is over;

9
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:24,771
its business is done.

10
00:00:24,772 --> 00:00:28,700
And then nothing moves. No one tells the
driver he is free to go. He waits.

11
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:30,020
Seven minutes.

12
00:00:31,220 --> 00:00:32,120
Eight.

13
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:33,720
A second cruiser rolls up,

14
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:35,820
a dog begins to circle the car

15
00:00:35,821 --> 00:00:36,350
—

16
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:37,540
and the whole night turns

17
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:39,380
on a question the Supreme Court would

18
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:40,748
take up three years later:

19
00:00:41,220 --> 00:00:42,852
not whether the stop was legal,

20
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:45,136
but whether it lasted too long.

21
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:46,840
We spend a lot of energy arguing

22
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:48,920
about when the police can pull you over.

23
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:50,820
This case is about something quieter,

24
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:53,757
and more personal —

25
00:00:53,758 --> 00:00:54,370
the clock.

26
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:56,100
Once an officer has a reason

27
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:58,220
to stop you, how many minutes does

28
00:00:58,220 --> 00:00:59,120
that reason buy?

29
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:01,281
Can they finish the ticket,

30
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:04,080
then keep you sitting there on
a hunch while they go looking

31
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:05,080
for something else?

32
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:08,340
In 2015, in a case called Rodriguez

33
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:08,890
v.

34
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:11,280
United States, the Supreme Court drew

35
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:12,180
a line —

36
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:14,271
not in space, but in time.

37
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:16,326
Over the next twelve minutes:

38
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:18,100
a traffic stop that ran a few

39
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:20,300
minutes too long, the weight the Court

40
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:21,520
hung on a single word,

41
00:01:21,980 --> 00:01:24,400
"mission," and why those seven or eight

42
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:26,660
minutes still shape every stop on every

43
00:01:26,660 --> 00:01:27,838
road today.

44
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:30,360
Just after midnight on March 27,

45
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:33,400
2012, Dennys Rodriguez was driving a Mercury

46
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:35,640
Mountaineer down a Nebraska highway

47
00:01:36,030 --> 00:01:39,684
when it drifted onto the shoulder
for a moment, then swung back.

48
00:01:39,685 --> 00:01:40,585
That was enough.

49
00:01:41,460 --> 00:01:43,860
Under Nebraska law, driving on the shoulder

50
00:01:43,860 --> 00:01:46,320
is a violation, and an officer named

51
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:47,820
Morgan Struble was watching.

52
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:50,609
Struble was no ordinary patrolman.

53
00:01:50,660 --> 00:01:52,020
He was a K-9 officer,

54
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:53,380
and riding in the back

55
00:01:53,380 --> 00:01:55,410
of his car was a trained drug-detection

56
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:56,402
dog.

57
00:01:56,680 --> 00:01:57,900
What happened next was,

58
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:00,340
for a while, completely routine.

59
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:02,810
Struble pulled Rodriguez over,

60
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,334
walked up to the window, and
did the ordinary things.

61
00:02:05,620 --> 00:02:06,936
He collected the license,

62
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:09,480
the registration, the proof of insurance.

63
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:11,107
He ran his checks.

64
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:13,146
He asked the usual questions.

65
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:18,226
Then he did the thing that, on paper, ends a
traffic stop: he wrote Rodriguez a warning,

66
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:19,647
not even a ticket,

67
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:21,000
just a written warning,

68
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,680
explained it, and handed the documents back

69
00:02:23,901 --> 00:02:24,730
.

70
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:26,813
Stop and notice what that moment means.

71
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:31,260
The reason for the stop was that the car had
touched the shoulder, and Struble had now dealt

72
00:02:31,260 --> 00:02:32,070
with it.

73
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:33,363
The warning was written.

74
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:34,976
The papers were returned.

75
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:38,660
In the language the Court would later
use, the "mission" of the stop

76
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:40,773
was complete.

77
00:02:40,774 --> 00:02:43,947
By any ordinary understanding, Rodriguez
was now free to drive away.

78
00:02:44,420 --> 00:02:45,320
He wasn't.

79
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:48,065
Struble never said, "You're free to go."

80
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:50,347
Instead he asked for something more —

81
00:02:50,660 --> 00:02:52,480
permission to walk his dog around

82
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:53,380
the car.

83
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:54,947
Rodriguez said no.

84
00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:56,860
And that refusal did not end

85
00:02:56,860 --> 00:02:57,760
the encounter.

86
00:02:57,900 --> 00:03:00,481
It started the clock this
whole case is about.

87
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:02,720
Because Rodriguez would not consent,

88
00:03:03,460 --> 00:03:06,060
Struble ordered him to shut
off the engine, step out,

89
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:07,365
and wait by the road.

90
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:09,740
He radioed for a second officer.

91
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:11,720
And for the next seven to eight

92
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:13,900
minutes, Rodriguez stood on the shoulder

93
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:14,880
of a dark highway,

94
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:16,740
no longer being ticketed,

95
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:18,880
no longer processed for anything at all,

96
00:03:19,260 --> 00:03:20,479
simply held.

97
00:03:20,820 --> 00:03:22,450
When the backup finally arrived,

98
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:26,400
Struble retrieved his dog and led it around
the Mountaineer. On the second pass,

99
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:28,660
the dog alerted, and a search turned

100
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:29,660
up methamphetamine.

101
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:32,845
From the first flash of the patrol
lights to that alert,

102
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:35,207
about twenty-nine minutes had passed,

103
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:36,820
and what the search found came

104
00:03:36,820 --> 00:03:40,576
in the stretch of time after
the warning, not before.

105
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:42,960
So here is the hard problem.

106
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:46,100
Everything Struble did up to the
warning was clearly legal.

107
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:47,540
Stopping a car that drifts onto

108
00:03:47,540 --> 00:03:49,308
the shoulder —

109
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:51,940
legal.

110
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:56,940
Checking a license — legal. A dog sniff, on
its own, the Court had said in an earlier

111
00:03:56,940 --> 00:03:58,466
case, is not even a "search".

112
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:01,240
Each piece, by itself,

113
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:01,700
was fine.

114
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:03,960
What turned this into a constitutional case

115
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:05,840
was the order they came in,

116
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:09,580
and the gap in the middle:

117
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:11,520
the officer finished the traffic stop,

118
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:13,160
then kept the driver there,

119
00:04:13,161 --> 00:04:16,869
with no ticket left to write, waiting for a dog.
The government's answer was, in essence:

120
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:17,580
what's the harm?

121
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:19,762
It was only seven or eight minutes.

122
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:24,520
Officers, it argued, should have a little
breathing room, a small buffer to run

123
00:04:24,520 --> 00:04:25,640
a dog around a car,

124
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:27,420
especially with a second unit

125
00:04:27,420 --> 00:04:28,578
on the way for safety.

126
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:30,700
A few extra minutes is such

127
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:32,490
a small thing, the government said,

128
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:34,680
that the Constitution shouldn't even count

129
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:35,350
it.

130
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:37,380
In legal terms, it was "de minimis"

131
00:04:37,433 --> 00:04:37,890
—

132
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:39,370
too slight to notice.

133
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:41,237
And that argument has real force.

134
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:43,120
Seven minutes is not long.

135
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:46,618
If you have ever idled at a red
light or in a drive-through,

136
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:49,640
you have surrendered more without
a second thought.

137
00:04:49,980 --> 00:04:50,980
Why should a brief,

138
00:04:51,060 --> 00:04:53,820
careful delay, done partly for the officer's

139
00:04:53,820 --> 00:04:56,480
own safety, suddenly count as a violation

140
00:04:56,480 --> 00:04:57,782
of your rights?

141
00:04:58,020 --> 00:04:59,960
On April 21, 2015,

142
00:05:00,240 --> 00:05:02,170
the Supreme Court gave its answer —

143
00:05:02,340 --> 00:05:03,380
six votes to three.

144
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:06,180
Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg wrote

145
00:05:06,180 --> 00:05:08,340
for the majority, and her reasoning turned

146
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:09,240
on that one word:

147
00:05:09,480 --> 00:05:10,330
mission.

148
00:05:10,360 --> 00:05:11,760
A traffic stop, she wrote,

149
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:13,748
is a seizure —

150
00:05:13,749 --> 00:05:16,852
for those minutes, the government
is holding you —

151
00:05:16,853 --> 00:05:21,360
and it is justified only by its purpose.
The stop exists to handle the violation

152
00:05:21,780 --> 00:05:23,880
and the safety concerns that ride along

153
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:24,780
with it:

154
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:27,280
checking your license, your registration,

155
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:30,340
your insurance, whether there
are warrants out for you.

156
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:32,034
Those tasks are the mission.

157
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:33,880
And the authority to hold you,

158
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:35,800
the Court said, ends when the tasks

159
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:38,582
tied to the traffic violation are —

160
00:05:38,583 --> 00:05:40,652
or reasonably should have been —

161
00:05:40,653 --> 00:05:41,553
completed.

162
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:43,560
A stop may last as long

163
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:44,480
as its job takes,

164
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:46,171
and no longer.

165
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:47,840
A dog sniff is not part

166
00:05:47,840 --> 00:05:48,740
of that job.

167
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:51,135
Sniffing for drugs, the Court noted,

168
00:05:51,220 --> 00:05:53,680
has nothing to do with keeping
a car safe on the road;

169
00:05:54,280 --> 00:05:57,500
it is a different errand entirely —

170
00:05:57,501 --> 00:06:00,081
ordinary crime detection, aimed
at finding evidence.

171
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:02,140
So when Struble held Rodriguez past

172
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:03,561
the warning to run the dog,

173
00:06:03,820 --> 00:06:05,980
he was no longer doing traffic-stop work.

174
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:10,727
He was doing something else, on the government's
initiative, with the driver's time.

175
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:15,340
Then the Court did the thing that makes the case
famous. It refused to let the outcome hang

176
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:16,900
on the order the officer happened

177
00:06:16,900 --> 00:06:17,800
to choose.

178
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:19,150
It would not matter,

179
00:06:19,180 --> 00:06:21,520
Ginsburg wrote, whether
the sniff came before

180
00:06:21,520 --> 00:06:23,060
the warning or after it.

181
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:24,720
One thing mattered:

182
00:06:24,900 --> 00:06:26,600
did holding the driver for the dog

183
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:30,375
add time to the stop?

184
00:06:30,376 --> 00:06:32,660
An officer cannot bank the minutes
he saves by working fast

185
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:33,800
and then spend them on an unrelated

186
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:34,700
investigation.

187
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:38,363
A stop, she wrote —

188
00:06:38,364 --> 00:06:40,027
quoting an earlier case —

189
00:06:40,028 --> 00:06:44,040
becomes unlawful when it is "prolonged beyond
the time reasonably required to complete

190
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:44,940
the mission."

191
00:06:45,240 --> 00:06:46,700
And that is why the government's

192
00:06:46,700 --> 00:06:49,020
"it was only a few minutes" argument lost.

193
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:52,880
The Court refused to measure the intrusion
by whether it felt big or small.

194
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:54,980
The question was not how long

195
00:06:54,980 --> 00:06:56,160
the extra delay ran,

196
00:06:56,560 --> 00:06:58,620
but whether the officer had any authority

197
00:06:58,620 --> 00:06:59,673
to impose it at all.

198
00:07:00,220 --> 00:07:01,590
Once the mission was finished,

199
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:02,520
he did not.

200
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:04,080
Seven minutes past the end

201
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:05,580
of a stop is still seven minutes

202
00:07:05,580 --> 00:07:07,220
the government had no reason to hold

203
00:07:07,220 --> 00:07:08,120
you.

204
00:07:08,320 --> 00:07:12,820
It is worth being precise about what the Court
did not do. It did not ban dog sniffs.

205
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:15,020
It did not ban traffic stops,

206
00:07:15,420 --> 00:07:17,000
or order officers to rush,

207
00:07:17,440 --> 00:07:19,240
or say the police can never act

208
00:07:19,240 --> 00:07:20,140
on a hunch.

209
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:22,540
And it did not set Rodriguez free

210
00:07:23,488 --> 00:07:23,788
.

211
00:07:23,789 --> 00:07:26,780
In fact, it left a second door wide
open. If Struble had a real,

212
00:07:26,780 --> 00:07:29,500
specific reason to suspect
Rodriguez of something

213
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:32,335
beyond the traffic violation —

214
00:07:32,336 --> 00:07:33,915
genuine reasonable suspicion —

215
00:07:34,380 --> 00:07:37,595
then holding him for the dog might
have been perfectly lawful.

216
00:07:37,920 --> 00:07:41,900
So the Court sent the case back to the
lower courts to answer exactly that:

217
00:07:42,020 --> 00:07:43,599
setting the timing rule aside,

218
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:45,880
did the officer have independent grounds

219
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:48,421
to keep Rodriguez waiting?

220
00:07:48,422 --> 00:07:50,780
The dissenters thought the
answer was plainly yes.

221
00:07:51,280 --> 00:07:55,900
Justice Clarence Thomas, joined by Justice Samuel
Alito, argued that the whole encounter

222
00:07:55,901 --> 00:07:56,410
—

223
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:57,756
all twenty-nine minutes —

224
00:07:57,860 --> 00:08:00,280
was reasonable, and that the officer had

225
00:08:00,280 --> 00:08:02,360
enough to justify the wait regardless.

226
00:08:03,280 --> 00:08:05,180
Alito added that the majority's rule

227
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:06,320
was strangely formal:

228
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:08,400
an officer who paused to call

229
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:10,242
for backup before running the dog —

230
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:11,923
a sensible safety step —

231
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:14,220
would now be penalized for the timing

232
00:08:14,221 --> 00:08:14,750
.

233
00:08:14,780 --> 00:08:16,500
Their point was not that time doesn't

234
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:17,370
matter.

235
00:08:17,400 --> 00:08:18,453
It was that a short,

236
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:20,140
careful delay by a lone officer

237
00:08:20,140 --> 00:08:21,614
at midnight was reasonable —

238
00:08:21,980 --> 00:08:24,937
which is the very thing the
Fourth Amendment asks.

239
00:08:25,140 --> 00:08:26,480
But the majority's line held,

240
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:27,420
and its strength

241
00:08:27,420 --> 00:08:28,473
is that it is clean.

242
00:08:29,020 --> 00:08:30,400
It does not ask a judge

243
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:32,140
to guess whether a delay felt long

244
00:08:32,140 --> 00:08:34,180
or short, or whether a hunch

245
00:08:34,180 --> 00:08:36,200
was strong enough to be worth a few minutes.

246
00:08:36,560 --> 00:08:37,981
It asks a simpler question:

247
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:40,790
is the traffic stop still doing traffic-stop

248
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:41,650
work?

249
00:08:41,680 --> 00:08:45,380
The moment the mission is finished, the
clock stops, and holding you any

250
00:08:45,380 --> 00:08:47,594
longer needs its own reason.

251
00:08:47,840 --> 00:08:49,660
That rule now sits behind millions

252
00:08:49,660 --> 00:08:50,780
of ordinary stops.

253
00:08:51,640 --> 00:08:53,520
Every time an officer finishes a ticket

254
00:08:53,520 --> 00:08:55,000
and then lingers —

255
00:08:55,120 --> 00:08:57,240
one more question, one more check,

256
00:08:57,820 --> 00:08:59,603
a slow walk around the car —

257
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:01,860
Rodriguez is the case that asks

258
00:09:01,860 --> 00:09:03,460
the quiet question underneath:

259
00:09:04,660 --> 00:09:05,713
is the mission over?

260
00:09:05,940 --> 00:09:06,900
Because if it is,

261
00:09:06,980 --> 00:09:08,500
the time that comes after belongs

262
00:09:08,500 --> 00:09:09,515
to you.

263
00:09:09,660 --> 00:09:11,260
So go back to that dark shoulder

264
00:09:11,260 --> 00:09:13,300
in Nebraska, just after midnight.

265
00:09:13,860 --> 00:09:15,123
The warning was written.

266
00:09:15,480 --> 00:09:16,954
The papers were handed back.

267
00:09:17,380 --> 00:09:19,780
The reason for the whole encounter
was finished —

268
00:09:20,020 --> 00:09:21,740
and still the driver could not leave

269
00:09:21,741 --> 00:09:22,370
.

270
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:24,420
The Supreme Court looked at those seven

271
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:26,040
or eight silent minutes and said

272
00:09:26,040 --> 00:09:28,093
they were not the government's to take.

273
00:09:28,180 --> 00:09:29,410
Your rights, it turns out,

274
00:09:29,440 --> 00:09:31,750
are not only about where the state can go.

275
00:09:31,780 --> 00:09:33,260
They are about how long it can

276
00:09:33,260 --> 00:09:34,274
keep you.

277
00:09:34,440 --> 00:09:36,280
And that is the thread running quietly

278
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:37,333
through all of this.

279
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:39,080
Three times now, we have watched

280
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:40,200
the law draw its line

281
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:41,305
in a different place.

282
00:09:41,780 --> 00:09:43,480
Around the home, and the walls

283
00:09:43,480 --> 00:09:44,901
that hold your heat inside.

284
00:09:45,260 --> 00:09:47,440
Around the person, and words spoken

285
00:09:47,440 --> 00:09:49,040
where no wall stands at all.

286
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:51,287
And now around time itself —

287
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:52,660
the minutes at the side

288
00:09:52,660 --> 00:09:53,760
of a road that no one

289
00:09:53,760 --> 00:09:55,286
is entitled to take from you.

290
00:09:55,760 --> 00:09:57,480
The same idea keeps surfacing

291
00:09:57,480 --> 00:09:58,427
in a new disguise:

292
00:09:59,200 --> 00:10:00,884
your freedom lives at the edges,

293
00:10:01,100 --> 00:10:03,220
in exactly the places the state would

294
00:10:03,220 --> 00:10:05,225
find it easiest to slip past.

295
00:10:05,420 --> 00:10:07,120
We call the Fourth Amendment a wall

296
00:10:07,120 --> 00:10:08,436
around your private life.

297
00:10:08,580 --> 00:10:10,360
But it may be truer to call

298
00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:12,701
it a line —

299
00:10:12,702 --> 00:10:15,780
drawn sometimes in space, sometimes
in sound, and here,

300
00:10:15,780 --> 00:10:16,680
in time.

301
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:18,400
Wherever it runs, the task

302
00:10:18,400 --> 00:10:19,300
is the same:

303
00:10:19,460 --> 00:10:21,240
to mark the point where the government

304
00:10:21,240 --> 00:10:23,060
has to stop, and you begin.

305
00:10:23,720 --> 00:10:26,567
That is where your rights have always
lived. Right at the edge.
